    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71236.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Right, no kidding. You can't think of very many parts of government that need security as much as the Attorney General's office, where they're looking at confidential information with regard to lawsuits and have a lot of information about individuals, residents of the state. I mean, this is the world we live in. We've seen through our elections, through attacks on credit card systems, through attacks on government now that that information is not as private as we would like to think it is. Kwame rules office has indicated and they think they are getting this under control. They've really said very little though and that's what's sort of I guess concerning here is we don't really have an idea of what has potentially been breached and what the costs of that potentially could be. But clearly the government in Illinois has problems when you have two major offices. getting attacked within a very close period of time. And it's something here, as in all areas of government, cyber security is something that needs to be very carefully intended to.",
