    "turn_text": "You know, and it certainly would seem that this is an issue that favors the Republicans from the political standpoint. Illinoisans will routinely say in polls they prefer a fairer, less partisan, more impartial and objective approach. to redistricting issues. But the question is, is there any price to be paid for defying that voter sentiment there? Governor Jay B. Pritzker may very well think that there isn't a price to be paid. And of course, the Democrats who have the vast majority of legislative seats probably willing to take their chances with it as well. You know, one little interesting footnote to this discussion because, you know, I think a lot of people probably think if she was on the other foot, if it was Republicans who had the overwhelming majority of the legislature and control of the governor's mansion and Democrats were scrambling for any sort of crimes that Republicans would be fighting tooth and nail to keep. their control of the process, and I'm sure they would say otherwise. But just as a little microcosm of this here in Sangamon County this week, our county board, which is overwhelmingly Republican, had a proposal before them from a Democratic County board member to adopt an independent commission modeled on the GOP proposal at the State House to draw the county district maps And that measure was sent back to committee rather than being voted upon by the Republican County Board. Gives you just a little idea of what might be happening if the roles were reversed here. But again, this is something that I think a lot of people would love to see. They would prefer that our legislative districts be drawn on the basis of what is in the best interest of voters rather than the best interest of politicians. But David, it sounds like at this point you are as skeptical as I am and a lot of people are that we're going to see much change.",
